      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 758,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, enabling phenomena like capillary action.<br>• **Density anomaly** – water is most dense at 4 °C; ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of it is saltwater in oceans). Freshwater (rivers, lakes, groundwater, ice caps) makes up only ~2.5 % of total water. |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and transport of nutrients occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Structural component** – cells are ~70 % water; water maintains cell turgor and shape.<br>• **Regulation of temperature** – through sweating, respiration, and water circulation. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), manufacturing (solvent, coolant), chemical synthesis, recreation, etc. |\n| **Environmental importance** | • Drives the **hydrologic (water) cycle**: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration.<br>• Influences weather patterns, ocean currents, and climate regulation.<br>• Supports ecosystems ranging from deep‑sea vents to alpine glaciers. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n1. **Polarity** gives it strong hydrogen‑bonding, which underlies many of its anomalous properties (high boiling point, surface tension, density behavior).\n2. **Hydrogen bonds** are transient but numerous, creating a dynamic network that can reorganize quickly—crucial for biological flexibility.\n3. **Versatile solvent**: It can dissolve salts, sugars, gases, and many organic molecules, allowing complex chemistry to happen in solution.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) whose physical and chemical traits—high heat capacity, surface tension, solvent power, and density anomaly—make it indispensable for Earth’s climate, geological processes, and all known forms of life. Its ability to exist in all three states under natural conditions and to cycle continuously through the environment underpins the planet’s ecosystems and human societies alike."
